\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Reverse norm signs and the Spin carrier}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative ring, let #{M} be an #{R}-module, and let
#{Q} be a quadratic form on #{M}. Write #{\widehat v=\iota_Q(v)} for a
Clifford generator. Before imposing any invertibility condition, consider the
two algebra-valued norm expressions
#{N_{\mathrm{rev}}(x)=\operatorname{rev}(x)x} and #{N_*(x)=x^*x}, where
#{x^*=\operatorname{rev}(\alpha(x))}. Identifying a scalar with its image in
the Clifford algebra gives

##{\begin{aligned}
N_{\mathrm{rev}}(\widehat v)&=Q(v),
&N_*(\widehat v)&=-Q(v),\\
N_{\mathrm{rev}}(\widehat u\widehat v)
 &=\widehat v\widehat u\widehat u\widehat v
 =Q(u)Q(v),
&N_*(\widehat u\widehat v)&=Q(u)Q(v).
\end{aligned}}

Thus the two signs differ on one generator and agree on a product of two.
More generally, reversal gives the product of the quadratic values on every
vector word
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.reverse_prod_map_ι_mul_prod_map_ι}, and the
two norm expressions agree on every even Clifford element
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.star_mul_self_eq_reverse_mul_self_of_mem_even}.}

\p{For a Clifford unit #{x}, membership in the even-unitary carrier is
equivalent to evenness together with #{N_{\mathrm{rev}}(x)=1}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.evenUnitaryGroup.mem_iff_reverse_mul_self_eq_one}.
Those two conditions do not establish Lipschitz membership, which must be
retained in the Spin criterion. Precisely,

##{\operatorname{im}\bigl(\Spin(Q)\longrightarrow
\operatorname{Cl}(Q)^\times\bigr)
=\operatorname{Lipschitz}(Q)\cap\operatorname{EvenUnitary}(Q)}

\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.range_spinGroup_toUnits}.
Consequently, if #{Q(u)Q(v)=1}, then #{\widehat u\widehat v} is an even
product of Lipschitz vector units with reverse norm one, hence a Spin element
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.ι_mul_ι_mem_spinGroup_of_norm_mul_norm_eq_one}.
This is an unquotiented scalar calculation. The passage to square classes,
including the odd reflection sign and its cancellation on even products, is
recorded separately in \ref{fcap-0023}.}

\p{Chevalley obtains the same norm from what he calls the main
antiautomorphism and records #{\lambda(v)=Q(v)} and the paired values
#{Q(u)Q(v)}
\citet{II.3.5--6, pp. 52--53}{chevalley1954algebraic}. His symbol #{\alpha}
in that passage denotes reversal, whereas #{\alpha} in \ref{fcap-0021}
denotes the grading involution. Meinrenken calls reversal transposition, writes
#{N(x)=x^\top x}, and defines Spin as its norm-one subgroup inside the special
Clifford group
\citet{Sections 2.2.6 and 3.1.1--2, pp. 34, 49--51}{meinrenken2013clifford}.
His convention has #{Q(v)=B(v,v)}, so his #{B} is the half-polar form #{b_Q}
of \ref{fcap-0027}. Both sources work in narrower finite-dimensional field
settings; Meinrenken here assumes characteristic zero and a nondegenerate
form. The formal identities linked above hold under the commutative-ring and
module hypotheses stated here.}
